## Part 6 — Memory Ownership

Two memory systems with different rules:

| System | Manages | Freed by |
|--------|---------|---------|
| GC (Hermes Hades) | JS objects, HostObject wrappers | GC finalizer on JS thread |
| Native heap | C++ objects, buffers | C++ destructors (RAII) |

**The boundary:** A `jsi::Object` backed by a HostObject — the JSI runtime holds a `shared_ptr<HostObject>`. When JS drops all references, GC runs `finalizeUnreachableObjects`, which drops the `shared_ptr`. If the ref count hits zero, the HostObject destructor runs on the JS thread.

**Ownership pitfalls:**

| Pattern | Risk | Fix |
|---------|------|-----|
| Background thread holds raw ptr to HostObject member | Use-after-free when GC collects | `shared_ptr` co-ownership |
| Lambda `[this]` from HostObject dispatched to bg thread | Dangling `this` | `[self = shared_from_this()]` |
| `CallInvoker` callback captures `jsi::Value` | Value invalidated before callback runs | Copy data to C++ types before dispatching |
| `shared_ptr` cycle (A owns B, B owns A) | Memory leak — never freed | Break with `weak_ptr` |
| Large native alloc invisible to GC | OOM (GC doesn't know to collect) | Call `runtime.instrumentation().collectGarbage()` as hint |

